To create a Discord.js v14 bot with a "ban" command as a slash command, follow these steps:

1. Install required packages: In your terminal or command prompt, navigate to the directory where you want to create your bot project and run the following command to install Discord.js and the required packages:

   ```bash
   npm install discord.js @discordjs/builders
   ```

2. Create the bot project: Initialize a new Node.js project by running the following command in your terminal or command prompt:

   ```bash
   npm init -y
   ```

3. Set up the Discord bot: Create a file named `index.js` in the project directory and add the following code to set up the Discord bot:

   ```javascript
   const { Client, GatewayIntentBits, SlashCommandBuilder, CommandsManageIntent } = require("discord.js");

   // Create a Discord client instance
   const client = new Client({
     intents: [
       GatewayIntentBits.Guilds,
       GatewayIntentBits.GuildMessages,
       GatewayIntentBits.DirectMessages,
       GatewayIntentBits.GuildMembers,
       GatewayIntentBits.MessageContent,
       GatewayIntentBits.GuildVoiceStates,
       GatewayIntentBits.GuildPresences,
       CommandsManageIntent
     ],
   });

   // Define the ban command as a Discord slash command
   const banCommand = new SlashCommandBuilder()
     .setName("ban")
     .setDescription("Bans a user from the server")
     .addUserOption((option) =>
       option
         .setName("user")
         .setDescription("The user to ban")
         .setRequired(true)
     )
     .addStringOption((option) =>
       option
         .setName("reason")
         .setDescription("The reason for the ban")
         .setRequired(false)
     );

   // Register the ban command
   client.on("interactionCreate", async (interaction) => {
     if (!interaction.isChatInputCommand()) return;
     if (interaction.commandName === "ban") {
       const user = interaction.options.getUser("user");
       const reason = interaction.options.getString("reason") ?? "No reason provided";
       try {
         // Ban the specified user
         await interaction.guild.ban(user.id, { reason });
         await interaction.reply({
           content: `User ${user.tag} has been banned from the server with the reason: \`${reason}\``,
           ephemeral: true,
         });
       } catch (error) {
         await interaction.reply({
           content: `Failed to ban ${user.tag}. Error: ${error}`,
           ephemeral: true,
         });
       }
     }
   });

   // Login the bot and listen for interactions
   client.login(process.env.DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN);
   ```

4. Provide your Discord bot token: Replace `process.env.DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN` with your actual Discord bot token in the `index.js` file. You can obtain a token by creating a Discord application and generating a bot token from the "Bot" tab of the application.

5. Enable Commands option in Discord Developer Portal: In your Discord Developer Portal, navigate to your application's "Bot" tab, then click on the "Click to Reveal Token" button. Enable the "Commands" option, and then copy the generated token value to be used as the environment variable `DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN` as shown in step 4.

6. Run the bot: With all configuration complete, you can now run the bot using the following command in the terminal or command prompt:

   ```bash
   npm start
   ```

Now, the Discord bot is ready to receive ban commands from users in any enabled Discord server where you have added the bot as a member. Users can invoke the slash command by typing `/ban` and following the prompts to specify the user to ban and optionally a ban reason. The bot will then attempt to ban the specified user and provide a confirmation or error message accordingly.

Here is a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text, taking a reference to a std::string as input and returning a modified std::string as output:

    ```cpp
    #include <string>
    #include <iostream>
    #include <regex>

    std::string convertMarkdownToHTML(const std::string& markdown_text) {
        // Define a regular expression pattern to match common markdown syntax
        static const std::regex markdown_regex(
            R"((?:^|[\n\r])(?:#*\s*)(.*?)(?:\s*#*));",
            std::regex_constants::icase);

        // Iterate through matched markdown syntax using a captured group
        std::stringstream html_text;
        for (std::sregex_iterator it(markdown_text.begin(), markdown_text.end(), markdown_regex), end_it; it != end_it; it++) {
            std::smatch match = *it;
            std::string markdown_text_block = match[1].str();
            std::string html_tag;

            if (markdown_text_block.empty()) {
                html_tag = "<br>";  // Match empty lines with a br tag
            } else if (markdown_text_block[0] == '#') {
                size_t num_hashes = std::count(markdown_text_block.begin(), markdown_text_block.end(), '#');

                // Convert markdown headers to HTML headers based on the number of hash symbols
                if (num_hashes == 1) {
                    html_tag = "<h1>" + markdown_text_block.substr(1) + "</h1>";
                } else if (num_hashes == 2) {
                    html_tag = "<h2>" + markdown_text_block.substr(1) + "</h2>";
                } else if (num_hashes == 3) {
                    html_tag = "<h3>" + markdown_text_block.substr(1) + "</h3>";
                } else if (num_hashes == 4) {
                    html_tag = "<h4>" + markdown_text_block.substr(1) + "</h4>";
                } else if (num_hashes == 5) {
                    html_tag = "<h5>" + markdown_text_block.substr(1) + "</h5>";
                } else {
                    html_tag = "<h6>" + markdown_text_block.substr(1) + "</h6>";
                }
            } else if (markdown_text_block[0] == '-' || markdown_text_block[0] == '*') {
                html_tag = "<ul><li>" + markdown_text_block + "</li></ul>";  // Convert bullet points to HTML unordered list
            } else if (markdown_text_block[0] == '>' && markdown_text_block[1] != '\n' && markdown_text_block[1] != '\r') {
                html_tag = "<blockquote>" + markdown_text_block.substr(1) + "</blockquote>";  // Convert blockquotes to HTML blockquotes
            } else {
                html_tag = markdown_text_block;  // Keep the text as is for plaintext or other markdown formats not matched
            }

            html_text << html_tag << std::endl;  // Append the HTML tag to the HTML stream
        }

        return html_text.str();  // Return the converted HTML string
    }
    ```

This function uses regular expressions to match common markdown syntax for headers (#), bullet points (- or *), blockquotes (>, not preceded by \n or \r), and empty lines. It then converts each markdown syntax into the corresponding HTML tag, such as `<h1>`, `<ul>`, `<blockquote>`, and `<br>`. Plain text and unrecognized markdown formats are preserved in the converted HTML. The function iterates through the matched markdown text blocks and appends the corresponding HTML tags to a `std::stringstream`, which is finally returned as a std::string containing the full HTML text.

